{:edb/db-before-t 810 :edb/db-after-t 811 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534123 50 #inst "2026-10-06T03:45:33.587Z" 13194139534123 true] [17592186115024 1100 "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x=5+4\\textrm{i}$. What is the other root?" 13194139534123 false] [17592186115024 1100 "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x=5+4\\textrm{i}.$ What is the other root?" 13194139534123 true] [17592186115024 1145 "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that\n\n$$\nx=5+4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate\n\n$$\nx=5-4\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis also a root." 13194139534123 false] [17592186115024 1145 "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that $x=5+4\\textrm{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate $x=5-4\\textrm{i}$ is also a root." 13194139534123 true] [17592186115025 1100 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3\\textrm{i}$. Given that the coefficient of $x^2$ in the equation is $2$, find the equation." 13194139534123 false] [17592186115025 1100 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-3\\textrm{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of $x^2$ in the equation is $2,$ find the equation." 13194139534123 true] [17592186115025 1145 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and\n\n$$\nx=-3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis a root, its conjugate\n\n$$\nx = 3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3\\textrm{i})(x+3\\textrm{i})= 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - (3\\textrm{i})^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 9(-1) &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 9 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots\n\n$$\nx=\\pm 3\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nbut the coefficient of $x^2$ is not $2$. Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2(x^{2} + 9) &= 0 \\\\\n2x^{2} + 18 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534123 false] [17592186115025 1145 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=-3\\textrm{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x = 3\\textrm{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3\\textrm{i})(x+3\\textrm{i})= 0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(3\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-9(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 3\\textrm{i},$ but the coefficient of $x^2$ is not $2.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $2.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}+9) & =0 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+18 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534123 true] [17592186115026 1100 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=6-8\\textrm{i}$. Given that the coefficient of $x^2$ in the equation is $-1$, find the equation." 13194139534123 false] [17592186115026 1100 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=6-8\\textrm{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of $x^2$ in the equation is $-1,$ find the equation." 13194139534123 true] [17592186115026 1145 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and\n\n$$\nx=6-8\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nis a root, its conjugate\n\n$$\nx=6+8\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nmust also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Bigl(x-(6-8\\textrm{i})\\Bigr)\\Bigl(x-(6+8\\textrm{i})\\Bigr) = 0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - x(6 + 8\\textrm{i}) - x(6 - 8\\textrm{i}) + (6 - 8\\textrm{i})(6 + 8\\textrm{i}) &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 6x - 8x\\textrm{i} - 6x + 8x\\textrm{i} + 36 - 64\\textrm{i}^{2} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 12x + 36 + 64 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - 12x + 100 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots\n\n$$\nx= 6 \\pm 8\\textrm{i}\n$$\n\nbut the coefficient of $x^2$ is not $-1$. Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-1$. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n- (x^{2} - 12x + 100) &= 0 \\\\\n- x^{2} + 12x - 100 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534123 false] [17592186115026 1145 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=6-8\\textrm{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=6+8\\textrm{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n\\Bigl(x-(6-8\\textrm{i})\\Bigr)\\Bigl(x-(6+8\\textrm{i})\\Bigr) = 0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(6+8\\text{i})-x(6-8\\text{i})+(6-8\\text{i})(6+8\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-6x-8x\\text{i}-6x+8x\\text{i}+36-64{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+36+64 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-12x+100 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x= 6 \\pm 8\\textrm{i},$ but the coefficient of $x^2$ is not $-1.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $-1.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-12x+100) & =0 \\\\ -{x}^{2}+12x-100 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534123 true] [17592186115204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277941 13194139534123 true] [17592186115204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277944 13194139534123 true] [17592186115204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277948 13194139534123 true] [17592186115204 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277965 13194139534123 true] [17592186115205 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277913 13194139534123 true] [17592186115205 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277951 13194139534123 true] [17592186115205 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277955 13194139534123 true] [17592186115205 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277972 13194139534123 true] [17592186115206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277920 13194139534123 true] [17592186115206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277927 13194139534123 true] [17592186115206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277934 13194139534123 true] [17592186115206 1020 #edb/ref 17592186277958 13194139534123 true] [17592186115588 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534123 true] [17592186115588 1100 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x =-1 + \\textrm{i}$. Given that the coefficient of $x^{2}$ in the equation is $1$, find the equation." 13194139534123 false] [17592186115588 1100 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=-1+\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $1,$ find the equation." 13194139534123 true] [17592186115588 1145 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and $x=-1+\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate $x=-1-\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-(-1+\\text{i}))(x-(-1-\\text{i}))=0\n$$\n\nBy expanding the parentheses, we can write the equation in standard form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-x(-1+\\text{i})-x(-1-\\text{i})+(-1+\\text{i})(-1-\\text{i}) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+x-x\\text{i}+x+x\\text{i}+1-{\\text{i}}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+1+1 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x+2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534123 true] [17592186115609 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534123 true] [17592186115609 1100 "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x = 3 - \\textrm{i}$. What is the other root?" 13194139534123 false] [17592186115609 1100 "A quadratic equation with real coefficients has a root $x=3-\\text{i}.$ What is the other root?" 13194139534123 true] [17592186115609 1145 "We're given that the quadratic equation has real coefficients and that $x=3-\\text{i}$ is a root. Therefore, its complex conjugate $x=3+\\text{i}$ is also a root." 13194139534123 true] [17592186115612 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534123 true] [17592186115612 1100 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x = 2\\textrm{i}$. Given that the coefficient of $x^{2}$ in the equation is $3$, find the equation." 13194139534123 false] [17592186115612 1100 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=2\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $3$, find the equation." 13194139534123 true] [17592186115612 1145 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=2\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-2\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2\\text{i})(x+2\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-(2\\text{i})^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-4(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm 2\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $3.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $3.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}^{2}+4) & =0 \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+12 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534123 true] [17592186277913 1017 #edb/ref 17592186277914 13194139534123 true] [17592186277913 1089 "q-160005" 13194139534123 true] [17592186277913 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534123 true] [17592186277913 1100 "A quadratic equation in the variable $x$ with real coefficients has a root $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}.$ Given that the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ in the equation is $4$, find the equation." 13194139534123 true] [17592186277913 1113 #uuid "c0e4e3f6-996e-5dd9-bca4-ac8109f31dab" 13194139534123 true] [17592186277913 1145 "Since the quadratic equation has real coefficients and  $x=\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ is a root, its conjugate  $x=-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i}$ must also be a root. Therefore, using the zero product property, we can write a quadratic equation with the required roots as follows:  \n\n$$\n(x-\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})(x+\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})=0\n$$\n\n   \n\nMultiplying out the parentheses and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{(\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i})}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}-\\frac{3}{2}(-1) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation has roots $x=\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{2}}\\text{ }\\,\\text{i},$ but the coefficient of ${x}^{2}$ is not $4.$ Therefore, to find the desired equation, we multiply the equation above by $4.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}) & =0 \\\\ 4{x}^{2}+6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534123 true] [17592186277914 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277915 13194139534123 true] [17592186277914 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277916 13194139534123 true] [17592186277914 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277917 13194139534123 true] [17592186277914 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277918 13194139534123 true] [17592186277914 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277919 13194139534123 true] [17592186277914 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534123 true] [17592186277914 1041 "selection" 13194139534123 true] [17592186277914 1104 #edb/ref 17592186277915 13194139534123 true] [17592186277914 1110 #uuid "f08fc1fe-886f-55ff-9c40-ae82cb5085de" 13194139534123 true] [17592186277915 1034 "4{x}^{2}+6=0" 13194139534123 true] [17592186277915 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534123 true] [17592186277915 1111 #uuid "d1762f5c-a828-593a-b476-2a0506e1ffc9" 13194139534123 true] [17592186277916 1034 "4{x}^{2}-4=0" 13194139534123 true] [17592186277916 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534123 true] [17592186277916 1111 #uuid "e36f6615-42cf-51c3-843c-23cbb69c46cb" 13194139534123 true] [17592186277917 1034 "4{x}^{2}+4=0" 13194139534123 true] [17592186277917 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534123 true] [17592186277917 1111 #uuid "0fae7175-8034-5d13-a670-ce76e142d890" 13194139534123 true] [17592186277918 1034 "4{x}^{2}+2=0" 131